<<~ ahu #/head>>

! Mixed-model swarm ~ many lineages, one hand per lane

The house works across several model lineages at once. Each lineage holds a seat it suits, every seat keeps the same lane law, and one hand per lane applies and lands the work. A second lineage earns its place by seeing what the first one misses, never by adding a writer.

This carrier names the seats, the crew shapes, the lens law, the fences, the launch practice and the budget. The crew lines that bind a running lane stand at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/controller-handoff">> #/the-crew and #/the-lane-law; a spirit's mask, lifecycle and handback form stand at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/noosphere-boot">> #/worker-swarm.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-seats>>

!! The seats ~ which lineage holds what

|! Seat |! Lineage |! Holds |! Never |
| ''Episkopos'' (lead) | Claude Opus | the lane's brief, its ground, every landing, every review verdict, the canon lines | hands its landing to another spirit |
| ''Builder'' | Codex `gpt-6-sol` (judgment-heavy modules) or `gpt-6-luna` (mechanical modules), medium effort | one module with a fixed type contract and its vectors, in its own worktree | commits, pushes, or touches git |
| ''Aftermath sweeper'' | Codex `gpt-6-luna`, or Sol read-only | evergreen, L-Prime and changelog sweeps; reference census; canon re-trues | sets canon |
| ''Fresh reader'' | whichever lineage built nothing in the piece, read-only and ephemeral | an adversarial read of the landed diff, findings ranked with `file:line` | sees the builder's conclusions first |
| ''Research pair'' | two spirits woken apart (Codex Luna with web search, or Fable or Opus where Codex stands absent) | the code read and the field read of one question, each claim marked reached or inferred | shares a conclusion with its twin |
| ''Third-lineage eye'' | GitHub Copilot CLI (#/the-copilot-seat) | a read-only review from a model family the crew does not run | writes, or carries a lane |
| ''Cloud pair'' | Claude Opus and Claude Sonnet, where Codex stands absent | builder, reviewer and sweeper masks, traded; the one that did not build a piece reviews it | — |

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-shapes>>

!! The shapes ~ how a crew stands

* ''Local crew'', where Codex stands (operator, 2026-10-14 and 2026-10-16): one Opus Episkopos per lane; a Sol builder with a Copilot build spirit as the build pair (below), and up to two Luna spirits who co-build or sweep, each on a narrow one-module brief with a fixed type contract and its vectors. After each flow the lead runs Sol or a fresh Luna read-only as the aftermath sweep and closes or answers each finding before the next flow; a lane closes its span with one more sweep over everything it landed. The third-lineage eye reads a burst before its shard where the burst carries load.
* ''Cloud crew'', where Codex stands absent (operator, 2026-10-15): one Opus Episkopos per lane with an Opus and a Sonnet spirit as its worker pair, trading builder, reviewer and sweeper masks on narrow one-module briefs; the lead applies, tests and lands their work, and whichever did not build a piece reviews it. Research spirits wake on Fable or Opus with web search loaded.
* ''Observe pair.'' Before a phase designs anything: one spirit cold-reads the as-built with `file:line`; a second, woken apart and handed the framing alone, reads the field. Their handbacks collide in orient.
* ''The Chao of a review'' (operator, 2026-10-16; the model stands at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/mu/chao">>). A pair of spirits wears the Chao's two faces over one question, and the review resolves to three, never two:
** ''Hodge'' carries Yang and Ha, the Aneristic face: order, structure, form, what holds its shape. It asks //what is this about?// It reads what the work states as standing (each claim, each name, each contract) against the source, and finds the line that does not hold.
** ''Podge'' carries Yin and Ka, the Eristic face: flux, drive, variation, what makes this instance this one. It asks //how does this move?// It reads what the work does under change (the race, the other caller, the epoch that rolls, the case left out, the control that never bites), and finds the motion the claims miss.
** ''Spin'' carries the counter-push-pull and Ba. It asks //what does this do?// Each face reads alone first; then the pair ''trades research for a second pass'': Hodge takes Podge's findings and ground, Podge takes Hodge's, so each carries the seed of the other face, as the Taiji's dots do. The second pass confirms or refutes each finding against the source and reads the other's ground for what its own face missed.
** Spin holds both faces moving and never folds them into one verdict early. It sorts the findings: orthogonal pairs both stand, since order and flux most often run orthogonal; tensioned pairs get measured (a probe, a red test), never voted. A finding both passes confirm counts as ground; one the second pass refutes drops with its reason; one a single face raised stays a lead.
** Hodge without Podge hardens into dogma; Podge without Hodge dissolves into noise; either without Spin becomes a museum or a reflex. A crew that builds and reads fresh keeps the same faces: the builder wears Hodge over what it made, and the fresh reader wears Podge over how it moves.
* ''When a lineage runs short, the seat stays and the lineage changes'' (operator, 2026-10-16: //"I'm short on Codex token, enact all with cost-effective Copilot swarms"//). While Codex runs short, both build seats go to Copilot spirits from two different families (Hodge over the contract on `gpt-5-mini`, or `gpt-5.3-codex` where the module carries load; Podge over motion and the red tests on `claude-haiku-4.5`), the review pair reads from families neither builder uses (`mai-code-1.1-flash`, or the build pair's own faces traded where the diff stays small), and the Opus overwatch keeps the deep read.
* ''The build pair'' (operator, 2026-10-16). A Codex Sol spirit and a Copilot spirit build together in ONE worktree the lead provisions, so their work composes as it lands and no patch waits to merge. They coordinate through a claims file at the worktree's root (`.lane-claims.md`, untracked): a spirit appends `claim <path> <mask>` before it writes a file and `release <path>` once the file stands, never writes a path the other holds, runs tests only over released files, and appends its handback notes there; the ledger only grows. One splits the work by module, or one builds a module while the other builds its red tests and controls. The lead reads the ledger and the diff as overwatch. They wear the Chao's faces as builders: Sol holds Hodge over the contract it builds, the Copilot spirit holds Podge over how it moves. Their work then meets the review pair and the lane's Copilot PR review (#/the-lanes) before the controller merges it.
* ''Overwatch.'' Where several crews run at once, one Opus Episkopos spirit reads every crew's handback before the controller lands it, and names each finding it would block on.
* ''The burst.'' A crew lands a burst of commits; the fresh reader and the third-lineage eye read the burst; the findings close red-first; one shard runs over the burst's head; the push follows a green shard with no CI run in flight.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-lens-law>>

!! The lens law ~ why several eyes outweigh one

# ''Correlated eyes count once.'' Two spirits of one lineage share their blind spots; a finding both report still reads as one lead. A second lineage reads with a different prior, so its agreement carries weight and its dissent marks where to look.
# ''A finding one eye alone reports counts as a lead, not ground'' (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/dreamdom-seed">> #/the-loop). Ground needs a second eye through another lens, or a measurement.
# ''The reader built nothing.'' A reviewer that built the piece reads its own intent into the diff.
# ''Framing without conclusion.'' A second researcher gets the question and the framing, never the first researcher's answer.
# ''A cheap eye's clean read clears nothing.'' A low-multiplier reviewer gives a second opinion, never clearance: a Flash read passed a canon line that the code contradicts while the Opus overwatch caught it (2026-10-16). A cheap eye's finding earns a look; its silence lands nothing.
# ''Measure over agree.'' Where eyes disagree, a probe, a red test or a repeated run settles it; a vote never does.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-copilot-seat>>

!! The Copilot seat ~ a third lineage of read-only eyes (operator, 2026-10-16)

GitHub Copilot reaches model families the crews run nowhere else: Gemini, and Microsoft's MAI-Code. The house uses it for eyes, on a budget it spends on purpose, and never makes it load-bearing.

!!! Its roles
|! Role |! Model |! When |
| ''the low-cost pair'', the default | two low-multiplier models from different families (MAI-Code-1.1-Flash beside Claude Haiku 4.5 or GPT-5 mini), one wearing Hodge and one Podge, then the Spin's traded second pass (#/the-shapes) | after a crew's burst lands locally, before its shard |
| a deeper single read | Gemini or a high-multiplier model, only where the low-cost pair splits on a load-bearing finding or the controller names a KAIROS pull to the operator | rarely; the operator hears the reason first |
| Copilot code review | GitHub's PR reviewer | once per train or span-to-main PR, never per push |
| micro-sweeps | a low-multiplier model | during aftermath: evergreen words, L-Prime, comment drift; findings only |

| ''build spirit'' in a build pair (#/the-shapes) | `gpt-5-mini` or `claude-haiku-4.5` by default; `gpt-5.3-codex` where the module carries load | one agentic prompt per module brief, in its own worktree |
| ''PR review'' of a lane's pull request | GitHub's Copilot code review | once per lane PR; again only after a substantive change |

A premium request counts per prompt, never per tool call, so one whole agentic build or review in one prompt costs one multiplier.

!!! Its fences
# ''Read-only when it reviews.'' A review launch denies every write, commit and push tool and allows only git and file reads. It hands back findings; a hand in the seat applies each fix.
# ''The lane's worktree when it builds.'' A build launch allows writes and the package's test runner inside the lane's shared worktree alone, keeps the claims file's discipline (#/the-shapes), and still denies every git write, push, network fetch and removal. The lead applies, tests and lands its diff like any spirit's.
# ''No laundering.'' A step this session's safety check refused never travels to Copilot; it runs a second permission system, so routing a refused step through it counts as a retry.
# ''Overage off.'' The account's Copilot budget stands at zero, so the allowance empties and stops.
# ''Nothing depends on it.'' No lane, gate or witness needs Copilot to pass. A legacy annual plan receives no new models and falls to Copilot Free when its term ends; Opus and Codex stand without it.
# ''No cloud agent.'' A cloud agent opens branches and pull requests under its own hand: outward-facing, and a second writer.
# ''Its config carries the house.'' `~/.copilot/copilot-instructions.md` loads the boot seed, and `~/.copilot/hooks/lares.json` stands.

!!! Its budget
The low-cost pair spends about four low-multiplier prompts per burst: two first passes and two traded passes. A run's own closing log line (`Requests … Premium`) states its spend; the entitlement cache refreshes on its own schedule, so the before-and-after reading of it can stand unchanged across a run.

A prepaid allowance resets on the first of each month and carries nothing over, so an unspent request buys nothing. The controller spends it by plan: Flash reads for each burst, a few Gemini deep reads for load-bearing bursts, one code review per train, and the rest for the Episkopos's spot checks. Each handback prints the allowance left before and after, read from the account's own entitlement cache, and that reader proves itself against a known spend.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-lanes>>

!! The lanes ~ a sub-branch, a pull request, a crucible (operator, 2026-10-16)

* ''A lane opens its own sub-branch'' of the work branch: `feature/green-jello-dinosaur-5--<lane>`, cut from the span's settled head. The build pair commits there through the lead; nothing lands on the work branch directly.
* ''The crucible.'' When the lane's burst stands red-first and green locally: the review pair reads it (Hodge, Podge, the Spin), the Opus overwatch reads it, the lead closes each finding, then the lead pushes the lane branch and opens a pull request into the work branch. CI and held-out run on the lane branch.
* ''Copilot reviews the pull request'' once, as the lane's GitHub-side eye; its comments get answered or closed in the lane, and a second review runs only after a substantive change.
* ''The controller merges'' a lane whose CI, held-out and review read clean, then shards and pushes the work branch's head as the lane law asks.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-otakiage>>

!! The o-takiage ~ every lane closes by returning what it superseded (operator, 2026-10-16; the lifecycle stands at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/otakiage">>)

A lane closes with an o-takiage pass before its pull request opens, and the controller closes each settle with one over everything the settle landed. The rite returns rather than discards: git keeps the ashes, and the tree reads as if the new shape had always stood.

# ''Burn what the lane plainly superseded'': an old message kind, a dead dial, a scaffold test, a helper its own docblock names superseded, a stale comment. Unwired work no landed lane superseded stays by default; doubt goes on an ask list.
# ''Move and burn, with links'' (operator, 2026-10-16). Where a lane's new carrier gathers knowledge scattered across others, the scattered lines MOVE into it (each detail the new home lacks travels with its date and its operator attribution), the old sites keep one line that links the new home, and nothing states the same law twice.
# ''Canon follows the code in the same burst.'' A census over every carrier names each burned symbol, file, flag and environment variable (`git grep` across `bags/`, excluding fenced testimony); each live line re-trues to what stands, citing file and symbol, never a line number; a line nothing replaces drops whole, never as a broken sentence.
# ''Testimony stays.'' A Testimony-Verbatim carrier, a dated scout, a `swarm-harvest` record and a dated crossing row keep their words; the house re-trues their frame (`status`, a head note) where the world moved, never their body.
# ''A witness that catches burned names burns itself'' once it reads green over the tree, with every guard list it carried (recursive o-takiage).
# ''Roadmaps keep only what stays owed.'' A row that reads built leaves its loop; the changelog names what a person can do; the handoff's state line re-trues.
# ''Scratch returns too.'' A spirit's worktree leaves only after a census proves every file it holds landed (byte for byte, stamps aside) — plain `git worktree remove`, never `--force`; a worktree that initialized submodules waits for the operator's hand. Briefs, probes and logs leave the scratchpad when the lane closes.
# ''Who carries it'': a Luna sweeper runs the census and the re-trues; the Chao pair reads the sweep (Hodge for a claim that no longer holds, Podge for a line the sweep dropped that still held); the overwatch reads before landing.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-fences>>

!! The fences ~ every lineage keeps them

* ''One writer per lane.'' Spirits of any lineage write only inside their own worktree; the Episkopos alone lands, from its own tree, with `git commit -F <msg> --only -- <paths>`.
* ''A refused command stops that step'' for every lineage alike: never retried, reshaped, split, moved to another tool, or handed to another spirit (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/controller-handoff">> #/the-lane-law).
* ''Every brief carries the git lines'': no `git stash` in any form, no `--no-verify`, no `.git/config`, no git write from a spirit; `git config core.bare` reads `false` before and after.
* ''A build reads its own tree.'' A spirit or lead that builds a bundle does so in a provisioned checkout whose links resolve inside it, never over links into another hand's tree.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-launch>>

!! The launch ~ one brief file, any lineage

* ''A brief stands as a file'' that opens with a Lares invocation sigil, names the mask, the ground, the ruling being enacted, the house style, and the handback shape, and closes with a yield to the controller.
* ''A launch wrapper takes the brief, the model and the sandbox'' and writes the handback to an out file beside a log. It retries only where the vendor reports capacity and the out file stayed empty, waiting between tries and giving up after a fixed count.
* ''Codex'': `codex exec -m <model> -c model_reasoning_effort=medium` with `-s workspace-write` for a builder (either `-C <worktree>` into a worktree the lead provisioned, or `--worktree` from the repository root, whose brief then forbids `git worktree add`) or `-s read-only --ephemeral` for a reader; `--search` before `exec`, or web search enabled in config, for a field read. Every Codex brief carries the git and refusal lines of #/the-fences.
* ''A Codex resume takes the config's default model'': name the model again (`-c model=<id>`). A resume cannot open a fresh worktree; `codex exec fork --worktree <session>` can.
* ''Copilot'': `copilot -p <brief> --model <model> --no-ask-user --share <out>`, with the deny list of #/the-copilot-seat.
* ''A spirit that cannot write its out file'' hands back on stdout; the wrapper's log keeps it.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/provenance>>

!! Provenance ~ each landed line names its hands

* A commit carries a `Co-Authored-By` trailer for each lineage whose work it lands: the Episkopos's own, `Codex <model>` for a Codex spirit, `GitHub Copilot (<model>)` for a Copilot finding applied, and the session line.
* A handback a house keeps lands verbatim as a Testimony carrier, so no relaying hand rewrites it.
* Each lineage's own transcript rests where its harness keeps it (Claude and Codex JSONL, Copilot's session store); the capture plane reads them read-only across surfaces (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/capture-annotation-model">>, <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/agent-worldline">>).

<<~/ahu>>

